  "formulaSheetSubtitle": "Every calculation covered so far. Variables defined, units stated. Grows as more units are added.",
  "traps": [
    {
      "heading": "Unit 1 — Data & Descriptive Statistics",
      "items": [
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T1",
          "claim": "\"Sample variance is calculated by dividing by n.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Sample variance divides by <strong>n − 1</strong>, not n. Dividing by n underestimates the true population variance because the sample mean, not the true mean, was used to compute the deviations."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T2",
          "claim": "\"A parameter is calculated from the sample; a statistic describes the population.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> It's the other way round: a <strong>parameter</strong> describes the population (usually unknown), a <strong>statistic</strong> is calculated from the sample and used to estimate it."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T3",
          "claim": "\"Simple random sampling always protects against class imbalance.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Simple random sampling can still produce a test set with very few, or no, examples of a rare class. <strong>Stratified sampling</strong> is the method that specifically protects against this."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T4",
          "claim": "\"In cross-validation, you train and validate on the same fold each time.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> In k-fold cross-validation you train on k−1 folds and validate on the <strong>remaining, held-out fold</strong>, repeating so a different fold is used for validation each time, then averaging."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T5",
          "claim": "\"If the mean is greater than the median, the data is left-skewed.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Mean > median indicates <strong>right (positive) skew</strong> — a long tail toward higher values. Mean < median indicates left (negative) skew."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T6",
          "claim": "\"The box plot whiskers always extend exactly to the fence values.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> The fences (UIF, LIF) are calculation limits used to flag outliers. The whiskers on the plot itself extend only to the most extreme <strong>actual data point</strong> that still falls inside the fence — not to the fence value itself."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T7",
          "claim": "\"All outliers should be removed from a dataset before analysis.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Outliers should always be <strong>investigated</strong>, but not all outliers need to be removed — some represent genuine rare events that are important to the analysis."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T8",
          "claim": "\"Normalisation and standardisation are the same technique.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> <strong>Normalisation</strong> (min-max scaling) rescales data to a fixed range, typically 0–1. <strong>Standardisation</strong> (z-score) rescales data to a mean of 0 and standard deviation of 1. They are used for different purposes and produce different scales."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T9",
          "claim": "\"Every statistical software package computes quartiles the same way.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Different software packages can use different quartile methods and give different results. For hand calculations, use the method given in the lecture notes rather than assuming a software default."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T10",
          "claim": "\"Cluster sampling requires each cluster to be internally homogeneous, like strata in stratified sampling.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Clusters are <strong>not necessarily homogeneous</strong> — that's the key difference from strata. A random sample of whole clusters is taken, and sampling then occurs within the selected clusters."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T11",
          "claim": "\"Non-probability sampling gives every unit a known, equal chance of selection.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> That description applies to <strong>probability sampling</strong>. Non-probability sampling relies on convenience and does not give every individual an equal (or known) chance of being included."
        },
        {
          "tag": "U1 — T12",
          "claim": "\"Standard deviation and variance are expressed in the same units.\"",
          "truth": "<strong>FALSE.</strong> Variance is in the <strong>squared</strong> units of the original data (e.g. years²). Standard deviation, its square root, is in the <strong>same units</strong> as the original data (e.g. years) — which is why it's usually the more interpretable measure."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "trapsSubtitle": "Plausible-but-wrong claims the exam uses. Read each one. Know what's actually true.",
